<< /A 59 0 R /Alt (Map graphic showing the location of a study area in China, with Jiangsu Province highlighted in yellow, Anhui in green, and Liaoning in blue. A red dot indicates the specific study area. An inset satellite image to the right displays detailed land cover and geographic features of the study region near coordinates 31°42'N, 118°39'-118°43'E. Scale bar and north arrows are present for both panels.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 60 0 R /Alt (Aerial photograph labeled A shows a dense forest canopy with uniform green foliage and minimal color variation. Aerial photograph labeled B displays a forest canopy with diverse tree species, including patches of lighter and reddish foliage. Aerial photograph labeled C presents varied shades of green in the forest canopy, featuring a prominent central conifer with some browning branches. Aerial photograph labeled D includes green forest alongside a light-colored dirt road and a parked red vehicle in the bottom left corner.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 61 0 R /Alt (Flowchart diagram illustrating a neural network architecture for object detection, with three main sections: Backbone, Neck, and Head. The Backbone processes input images through convolutional and pooling layers, the Neck uses concatenation and upsampling to combine features, and the Head outputs detection results at four different spatial resolutions using separate detect blocks. Component details and connections are visually organized with color-coded blocks and arrows for each operation, and smaller diagrams at the bottom show the internal layers of key components such as SPPF, Bottleneck, C2f, and Conv modules.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 62 0 R /Alt (Diagram compares four network modules: attention, DCNv3, convolution, and DCNv4, illustrating their response windows, color-coded pixel values, query and response pixels, and noting each module's window, weight, and value range properties in accompanying labeled boxes.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 63 0 R /Alt (Two-part diagram compares memory access costs in DCNv3 and DCNv4 using 3D tensor blocks with color-coded channel groups. DCNv3, at left, uses six threads for memory access, while DCNv4, at right, uses three, achieving twice fewer memory access costs.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 64 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating a neural network block with an initial one-by-one convolution, split operation, multiple stacked deformable convolutions with group and expansion parameters, intermediate additions, concatenation, and a final one-by-one convolution, featuring skip connections throughout the structure.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 65 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrates a deep learning feature extraction process, beginning with an aerial image, followed by sequential downsampling blocks, fusion with FPN and PAN modules, and outputting multi-scale feature maps at four different sizes labeled P2 to P5.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 66 0 R /Alt (Flowchart diagram of a neural network architecture showing input passing through group convolution, pooling, concatenation, sigmoid functions, and re-weighting, followed by cross-spatial learning with normalization, pooling, softmax, matrix multiplication, summation, and final sigmoid and re-weighting steps producing output.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 67 0 R /Alt (Diagram showing three stages of block matrix addition and partitioning along both axes, with colored blocks labeled by dimension sizes M, N, and G, arrows indicating divisions, and plus symbols indicating summation.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 46 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 68 0 R /Alt (Figure with two line charts comparing YOLOv8n and MSDF-Net models over 200 epochs. Panel A shows loss decreasing for both models, with YOLOv8n achieving lower loss values. Panel B depicts mAP@0.5 increasing, with MSDF-Net reaching higher values than YOLOv8n.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 47 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /A 69 0 R /Alt (Panel A shows an aerial photograph of a forest with dense green foliage and some trees displaying autumn colors. Panel B displays the same forest image overlaid with faint blue heatmap regions and concentrated yellow and red spots scattered throughout. Panel C features a similar overlay but with more distributed and intense blue, green, yellow, and red areas, indicating a denser or more widespread focus compared to Panel B.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 50 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /A 70 0 R /Alt (Six panels show aerial drone images of tree canopies labeled with colored bounding boxes. Comparison between \215Ground Truth\216 and \215Output Diagram\216 rows illustrates model detection performance for tree disease stages, backgrounds, and vegetation densities.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 52 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
28 0 obj
<< /A 71 0 R /Alt (Six-panel comparison graphic showing aerial images of forest canopies. The top row labeled \215Ground Truth\216 displays color-coded bounding boxes for different case types: class confusion, small-target misses, and edge-clipped false positives. The bottom row labeled \215Output Diagram\216 shows corresponding detection results with additional boxes for true positives, false negatives, and false positives, indicating performance differences for each case.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 53 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
